    "media:imagetext": "#6\nWe have been here now two and one half days with no air attack\nwhich probably means a strong one or more tomorrow, unless of course,\nthis weather continues.\nThere is an air of expectancy in Nanking, a\nsort of indefinite tenseness which must get on everyone's nerves\nsooner or later.\nPossibly they get used to it and probably I would\ntoo, but frankly, I'll be glad to get away.\nW1ll send this letter by Tuesday's plane to Hankow with the\nhope that it will catch Eurasia to Hongkong Wednesday and the Clipper\nto Manila on Thursday. Next installment will follow later.\nWe get little news here except about the Shanghai front and\neven that is mixed with all sorts of wild rumors.\nWeren't the Japs\nshowing their real nature when they fired at the\n\"doomed battalion\"\nwhen they evacuated that warehouse and surrendered to the British\nfor internment? Those dirty little Japs haven't any decent traits.\nWhy didn't they cease firing and give those Chinese a rousing cheer\nwhen they evacuated? That would have been sporting - but not at all\nJapanese.\nTuesday, November 2,1931\nIf I remember correctly, this letter was started with the state-\nment that we were\nembarking on a trip which might prove exciting.\nProbably I too was a victim of newspaper stories which depict a war\ntorn China, the air filled with Japanese planes raining bombs on\ndetenceless Chinese cities. As a matter of fact, we flew across 600\nmiles of China skies from Hongkong to Hankow without seeing a Japanese\nplane - and now after more than a week in war torn China we have yet\nManking that hundreds of bombe have been\nEven here at\nis difficult to\nfind any trace of damage.\nThis morning we had an air raid alarm, but\nsoon the \"all clear\" signal was given, and no planes or bombs were\nheard.\nWednesday,\nNovember 3rd.\nNo excitement today except one air rala\nWalked over to the Embassy office across the road from Mr. Peck's\nand inspected the U.S. dug out - very muddy, dark and full of\nmosquitos.\nThink when the raids come I'll take my chances outside.\nA direct hit will get you either in or out of that hole in the ground.\nIf you are outside and lie flat, you are just about as well off and\nlots more comfortable.\nBond left tonight by motor car for Shanghai.\nMany cars are constantly going back and forth, mostly at night, in\norder to avoid Jap airplanes machine-gunning them on the road.\nfriend of ours left his car as a plane approached and ran out\nfield where there was a Chinese grave mound.\nThe plane dove at him,\nbut he stayed in the lee of the mound until it passed - then he noticed\nthe Jap turn and come back at him, so he ran around the other side of\nthe mound - again in the lee. This performance was repeated during six\nattacks, after which the Jap flew off, probably out of ammunition.\nI hope Bond doesn't have any such experience. He is coming back on\n2-8801/S/PC\n- 6-",
